On August 25th, three AI agents edited the same checkout at once. I'd started one coding session, and it helpfully handed sub-tasks to helper agents — in its own working directory. Two of them touched the same README. When the dust settled, one agent's commit had quietly swallowed the other's edit. No conflict, no error, just missing work I only found by reading the diff.

That's a bug a team makes. Two engineers on one branch, nobody talking. The agents didn't invent a new failure mode; they reproduced an old one at team speed.

The [Silicon Valley Bicycle Coalition](https://bikesiliconvalley.org "Silicon Valley Bicycle Coalition") is leaving Salesforce for Monday.com, and I built the glue. **TODO:** one line on volunteer vs. paid, since it changes the "one engineer" framing.

## What got built

Salesforce does a lot for a nonprofit for free, or for an AppExchange plug-in fee. Monday doesn't. Each gap became a [Cloudflare Worker](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/ "Cloudflare Workers docs") — 17 of them.

**TODO:** ~6-row table, Worker → what Salesforce used to do. Candidates from the notes: donation rollups, Mailchimp sync, Funraise intake (polled hourly for recurring gifts, since its webhook only fires on transactions), calendar ICS feeds, the token broker, the staff portal.

By the numbers, as of November 2nd:

- 1,259 non-merge commits since February, almost all of them from August on.
- About 85% of them carry a `Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer.
- ~63k lines of test TypeScript against ~56k lines of everything else. There's more test than code.
- 25 dated design specs, one per real decision.

**TODO:** link the repo once it's public on GitLab. This post can't ship before that.

## One shape, seventeen times

Almost every Worker looks the same: a webhook arrives, gets deduplicated in D1, goes onto a Queue, and a consumer writes it to Monday. Failed writes land in a [dead letter queue](https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/dead-letter-queues/ "Cloudflare Queues: dead letter queues"), which means a failure is a replayable backlog, not a silently dropped donation.

