## The guardrails, and the incidents that wrote them

Every rule in the repo's `CLAUDE.md` has a date behind it. That's the point of this post.

**One worktree, one agent, ever.** The August 25th story above. Each agent gets its own [git worktree](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree "git-worktree documentation") and its own branch.

**Look before you `worktree remove`.** Also August 25th. Removing a worktree silently deleted its gitignored `data/` folder: the load rehearsal's only Salesforce-to-Monday ID ledger, the geocode cache, and a pile of human-reviewed approvals. Now every agent runs `git status --ignored` first.

**The primary checkout is for `main`.** On September 20th and 21st, two separate sessions committed feature work straight into my main checkout, and a merge request went in with an unrelated commit attached. A [pre-commit hook](https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks "githooks documentation") now refuses any non-`main` commit there. Prompts are suggestions; hooks are rules.

**Know your free tier.** In early September, D1's [100,000 writes per day](https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/limits/ "Cloudflare D1 limits") on [Workers Free](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/ "Cloudflare Workers pricing") stalled the rollups consumer with 2,865 rows stuck. The fix was two-part: pay the five dollars, and only write rows that actually changed.

**Bot protection doesn't know what a calendar app is.** From September 25th to October 2nd, [Super Bot Fight Mode](https://developers.cloudflare.com/bots/get-started/super-bot-fight-mode/ "Cloudflare Super Bot Fight Mode") served calendar apps a challenge page instead of an `.ics` file. Nobody's calendar complained; it just stopped updating. A WAF exemption fixed it. That rule lives in the dashboard, not the repo, so the README documents it.

**Never call a token scoped without measuring it.** On September 30th I checked what a Monday.com API token could actually reach. A user in *no* workspace could still see 212 boards and edit 199 of them. So no Worker holds a Monday token at all: a broker Worker hands out short-lived access from three OAuth apps split by scope, and the public portal can never hold write scopes. **TODO:** decide whether this becomes its own short post.

**Some bugs only show up in production data.** Monday's [delete webhooks](https://developer.monday.com/api-reference/docs/webhooks "monday.com webhooks") carry an `itemId`. Every build before October 12th rejected them, so deleted gifts never left donor totals. No unit test would have caught it, because the tests were written from the same wrong assumption.

**Build an off switch before you need it.** On October 26th each Worker got a write switch in [KV](https://developers.cloudflare.com/kv/ "Cloudflare Workers KV"): `on`, `hold`, or `off`. Hold keeps work queued; off drops it on purpose. A 22-hour hold later drained 11 gifts in 37 seconds.

A few more that earned a line each:

- Monday quietly serves a deprecated [API version](https://developer.monday.com/api-reference/docs/api-versioning "monday.com API versioning") as whatever the current maintenance version is, so every Worker pins one.
- The GraphQL client is copied into each Worker rather than shared, so each one deploys standalone and stays forkable. CI checks the copies are byte-identical, so they can't drift. Duplication you can't get wrong beats a shared package you have to version.
- Sandbox Workers can only receive. They can't reach production sinks, and the [send-email allow-list](https://developers.cloudflare.com/email-routing/email-workers/send-email-workers/ "Cloudflare: send emails from Workers") means a sandbox can only email a sandbox address.
- A CI check fails the build if a log line could contain an email address.

## What a skeptic should ask

**Who maintains 56,000 lines of agent-written TypeScript after I leave?** This is the weakest point, and I'll say so. The answer I have: every Worker has a README that doubles as its spec, staff have UAT scripts, and the whole thing is configured rather than forked to run for another org. **TODO:** be honest about what's still missing.

**Why not Zapier, Make, or a Monday marketplace app?** **TODO:** the real answer — cost, reliability, the DLQ/replay story?

**Isn't 85% AI-written code a liability for a donor database?** It would be without the rules above. Donor data lives in a gitignored folder, logs are checked for PII, the sandbox can't touch production, and no Worker holds a credential it doesn't need.

